---
// Determinism (#416 (engine and player)): one seed run twice, side by side,
// event for event. The recorded runs are src/runs/determinism.json (written
// by `cargo test -p moonpool-wasm-demo --test site_runs`); public/compare.js
// runs them again in the engine.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Technique from "../../../layouts/Technique.astro";
import { url } from "../../../nav";
import { repoFn } from "../../../lib/source.mjs";
import runs from "../../../runs/determinism.json";

const main = repoFn("crates/moonpool-wasm-demo/src/bin/sim_register.rs", "main");
const [first, next] = runs;
---
<Technique
  title="One thread, one clock, one seed"
  description="Run a seed two times. You get the same run, event for event."
  guide={[
    "part2-foundations/01-determinism.md",
    "part2-foundations/02-single-core.md",
    "part2-foundations/03-seeds.md",
    "part2-foundations/11-executor.md",
  ]}
>
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>A distributed system fails on a rare order of events: a message that comes late, a crash between two
    writes. On real machines, the scheduler, the network and the clock make this order. You cannot make it
    again. A test that failed one time and then passes tells you nothing.</p>

  <h2>The technique</h2>
  <p>moonpool runs all the processes of a test on one thread, with one executor. The network, the disk and the
    clock are simulated. One stream of random numbers makes each choice: the order of the tasks, the latency
    of each message, each fault. A seed starts this stream. Thus one seed gives one run.</p>
  <p>A sleep does not wait. When all the tasks wait, the clock jumps to the next event. Seconds of simulated
    time take milliseconds of real time.</p>

  <h2>The live run</h2>
  <p>The left run and the right run use the same seed. Your browser runs both, and each event on the right is
    the same as the event on the left at the same step. Give the right run another seed: the first event that
    is not the same gets the <b>≠</b> mark. The arrow keys step the two runs together.</p>
  <div class="compare breakout" id="compare">
    <form class="compare-ctl run" id="compare-form">
      <label class="label" for="seed-a">left seed</label>
      <input id="seed-a" inputmode="numeric" pattern="[0-9]*" maxlength="15" autocomplete="off" value={first.config.seed} />
      <label class="label" for="seed-b">right seed</label>
      <input id="seed-b" inputmode="numeric" pattern="[0-9]*" maxlength="15" autocomplete="off" value={first.config.seed} />
      <button class="btn small" type="submit">Run both</button>
      <button class="btn small" type="button" id="seed-next">Right: next seed</button>
      <p class="compare-out" id="compare-out" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </form>
    <div class="compare-runs">
      <section class="run" id="run-a" aria-label="The left run"></section>
      <section class="run" id="run-b" aria-label="The right run"></section>
    </div>
    <noscript><p>The live run needs JavaScript.</p></noscript>
  </div>
  <script is:inline type="application/json" id="runs" set:html={JSON.stringify([first, next]).replace(/</g, "\\u003c")} />

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>The small system of this page is also an example simulation of the repository
    (<code>cargo xtask sim run register</code>). <code>check_determinism()</code> runs each seed two times and
    fails the seed when the two runs do not make the same random draws:</p>
  <Code theme="css-variables" lang="rust" code={main} />
  <p>In a test, <code>set_debug_seeds(vec![seed])</code> and <code>set_iterations(1)</code> replay one seed
    alone.</p>
  <script is:inline type="module" src={url("compare.js")}></script>
</Technique>
